Indian
Let’ s start with two beliefs from India:
Intrinsic
Buddhism teaches that no unchanging soul or permanent identity exists
within a human being. Rather , when we hear our thoughts, what we mistake
Ú “me” who thinks them, for a conscious self, is in fact a mirage, a
nonself. In short, Â exists ế self is an illusion.
Extrinsic
Hinduism proposes the opposit e belief: The self exists but Â is an
illusion. The ground for what is is unknown and unknowable. Maya, the
power of illusion, gives form to  formlessness but conceals and distorts the
source. The world we experie nce with our senses exists bu t as a pale
imitation ¤ ultimate Â  that hides behind Maya’ s illu sions. This
realm is unexplainable because language itself is a by-product of Maya.
Gr eek
Greek philosoph y , developing at about ¶ time, contradicted both of
these beliefs:
Intrinsic
Socrates, in contradiction to Buddhism, ar gued ø self, far from being
an illusio n, sits ² center of e xistence. “The unexamined life ī worth
living,” he taught, therefore, “know thyself.” How can we hope for wisdom
of th e w orld that surrounds us until we come to terms with ặ inside
us? How  can we understand o ther š until we perceive the
Ä within ourá?
Extrinsic
Aristotle, in contradiction to Hinduism, believed that ặ is both real
and kno wable. A t heart, we are  political animals and our natu ral home is
society . Fulfillm ent consists £ active, public exercise of our finest
faculties aimed ² betterme nt of others. The well-lived l ife displays
virtue, excellence, and distinctively rational thought in a like-minded
community , extended over a lifetime.
Chinese
Chinese philosophy parallels both the Greek ª Indian:
Intrinsic
T aoism, like Socrates and Buddha, looked out on Â from  with£
self. This philosophy emphasizes living in harmony Ñ substance and
patterns ¤ n atural, rather than social, world. T aoism does not call for
elaborate public rituals, but rather simplicity , spontaneity , compassion, and
humility—behavior in accordance Ñ inner spirit of nat ure. T aoism
teaches that mastering others requires force, while masteri ng the self
requires strength; knowing others is wisdom, while knowing  yourself is
enlightenment.
Extrinsic
Confucius, like Aristotle and Hinduism, believed that well-being depends
on a well-order ed society . Confucianism takes society’ s ấ view to
emphasize public āity , fam ily loyalty , ancestor veneration, and a strict
hierarchy in which elders are re spected İir children, husbands İir
wives, and in all cases, justice prevails.
Nineteenth Century
T wo tho usand, five hundred years later , philosophy remained split along the
same divide:
Extrinsic
Karl Marx, like Aristotle, believed that social forces determine
consciousness. Like Buddha, Marx felt that a fixed self does not exist: “All
Îtory is a continuous transformation of human nature.”
Intrinsic
Sigmund Freud reversed Marx. In h½ory , the µ mind
commanded the center ¤ universe; social structures beyond the family
are irrel evant. H is model of inn er life comprised three components: id (the
source o f life’ s ener gy or libid o), ego (the self or ö id entity), and
superego (the conscience). Psychoanalysts since Freud have ar gued about
which, if any , ạ three components ½ true self.
T wentieth Century
Extrinsic
ë mid-twentieth century , anthropologist Joseph Campbell invented his
radical theory of myth. He took Carl Jung’ s belief in µ
primordial archetypes and cas t them as roles in an all-purpose story .
Campbell claimed that his monomyth had been passed ậ ages
from cu lture to culture, when in fact it was more invention th an legend.3
Belief in this m onomyth led many writers to reduce action characters to
dimensionless clichés.
Intrinsic
Psychologist W i lliam James viewed inner life as an ongoing contradiction:
At times the private self ü like a solid inner awareness, ến
daydreams and confusions liquefy consciousness into ever -changing
streams of images and impressions. James wondered, “How can it be that
each of us has both a ö an ongoing singular identity , plus a ö
the very ¹ á we used to be?” How can we be the one person we
know and all th e former á that litter our memories? The se Jamesian
