around i t but is self-aware—a m ind that can step back from itself and see
itself as an object.2
The natu re ¤ inner self has been debated for centuries. Is it  a Â
or a fantasy? D oes a mind staring at itself see itself or mi rror images
reflecting into infinity?
Buddha, as we noted, believed t h² self is illusionary becaus e ¾
inside our minds began as sens ory impressions of sights țunds from
outside our minds. What we ca ll the self is actually a bundle of external
imprints that appear one after th e other like an actor coming and going on-
and of fs tage. Th erefore, a true se lf does not exist—it’ s an anatta  (nonself), a
sort of mental special ef fect.
Socrates reverse d this. He believed that not only do š have
a so lid inner re alm but two se lves living within it: the observer ª
observed. The cor e self  (the observer) ½ epicenter of consciousness that
watcheắ go by and tries to u nderstand it. The ð send s the agent
self  (th e observed) into ặ to take action. The core s elf then
perceives its agent self in action  and À an ­ to its own daily
life. This is self -awareness—the performance in your inner the atre that no
one attends but you.3
Consider this: After doing something foolish, û you often think the
thought, “Y ou idiot!”? When you do, who, exactly , is shaming who? When
you man age a success, you often think, “I got that right.” Wh o is patting
whose back? How do self-criticism and self-congratulation work? Who
talks to whom?
As y ou read this  page, an observing awareness within you follo ws your
every m ove. Y ou  perceive (watch yourself read), Đ act (take notes on
paper or store them in memory). This pivot from awareness-to-action-to-
awareness within œ separates the ð ´ agent self.4
Some neuroscie ntists, è to this day , still side with Buddhism.
They ar gue that because each region ¤ brain has a separate function and
because no single region, in and of itself, causes self-awareness , a ð
does not exist.5
Others agree with Socrates: Human ò have motor neurons for
movement, sensory neurons fo r the senses, and interneurons—by far the
most num erous—Ú heavy lifting of thought. The brain, wit h its residue
of past experiences and imaginings of future happenings, coupled Ñ
body’ s nervous system ª totality of its moment-by-moment sensory
encounters, focu ses billions of impulses Ö the center of aw areness, the
ð. Self-awareness, therefore, ½ side ef fect of all the neurons in
all these regions working in concert. And that’ s why damage to any one
region ¤ brain diminishes or extinguishes the ö self. Peak self-
awareness, watching and doing, radiates ậ well-nourished brain
of a healthy body .6
This īhing new . The Eg yptians saw the observing self as a
protective spirit  and named i t the Ba Soul; the Greeks called it the
Daemon;7 t he Rom ans du bbed it the Geni us.8 But if, ² end ¤ day ,
science decides ø self is a  fiction, as Buddha claimed, fine with me.
Then it’ s our fiction, our nature, what makes us human.9
(T ry this: Go to a mirror and glance into your eyes. For an instant you’ll
sense so meone else, deep within, looking back. W ith the next blink,
è you’ll realize that wha t you glimpsed was a split-second image of
