T H E  O U T E R  C H A R A C T E R
Human ò, as social anim als, need to manage the impre ssions they
make on others. They do so by playing a great variety of  roles. The
evolutionary pu rpose ạ r ecitals is to get along, get ahead, and get
laid. Because their survival depended on stellar performances, our
ingenious ancestors evolved consummate skills for imitation and
expression. In short, humans are now , and always have been, actors.
This ß mean we’re insincere. It’ s simply the commonsense
realization that as we move from situation to situation, we subtly shift from
self to self, depe nding ¯ re lationship ¤ moment: priest/confessor ,
boss/employee, wife/husband, stranger/stranger . A human being can swing
from acting like a child to acting like a lover to acting like a New Y orker , or
all three at once.
So once you have circled the orbits of your cast galaxy , you will need to
bring ea ch char acter to life. In  this chapter we explore the composite of
vivid social and personal á that make first and ongoing impressions on
readers ő. This  collection of outer -facing behaviors,
mannerisms, and personality traits À the role’ s Characterization .
