R E A L I S T I C  V E R S U S  S Y M B O L I C
C H A R A C T E R S
Realism and Symbolism create characters at opposite ends ¤ cast
spectrum—one r ooted in fact, th e other in abstraction. In betwee n these two
extremes run the mixtures and mer gers of qualities that make possible every
character ever imagined. So, let ’ s separate these two poles to see wh²y
are and how they influence character creation.
The Symbolist tradition began in prehistoric myths. Mankind’ s earliest
storyå symbolized the forces of nature (sun, moon, lightning, thunder ,
seas, mountains ) into gods, demigods, and other supernatural ò. In
these m yths, gods create the cosmos and, with it, š.
Paraphrased from epoch to epoch, culture to culture, myths trav el mouth to
ear with  slight loss of meaning because they are not literature. In myths,
words mean little, symbolic characters and actions everything.
The Realist tradition dates back  to Homer ’ s vivid depictions of  warrior
psychology and  blood-spattere d battles £ Iliad . Re alism seeks to
portray life as lived, unclouded by false beliefs or sentimental
beautifications. Its straightforward dramatizations avoid symbolic dream
fiction to emphasize hard-edged thought fiction—characters living £
as-is rather than the as-wished-for .
The key  dif ferences between realistic and symbolic characters number at
least ten:
1. T ruth: Factuality · W ish Fulfillment
Realism grapples » factual world in which wishes almost never come
true; whereõ symbolic genres of speculative fiction—myths and
fantasies, both ancient and modern—put wish fulfillment Íction. The
heroes and superheroes of today , with or without magical powers, live in a
strictly ā universe where  Good always triumphs over Evil, love
conquers all, and death does not end life.
Other symbolic genres, ° fables and legends, treat wishes  as dark
and dangerous, so they tell cautionary tales that teach ā lessons. “The
Fox ª G rapes” by Aesop, “Hansel and Gretel” by th e Brothers
Grimm, ª legend ¤ H ouse of Atreus are three classi c examples.
Modern fables warn of a dys topian future: Geor ge Orwell’ s Nineteen
Eighty-Four , Mar garet Atwood’ s The Handmaid’ s T ale , Naomi Alderman’ s
The Power .
2. T rue Character: Complex Psychology · Imposing Personality
Symbolic ås incite conflicts between characters rather than
contradictions within characters. As a result, symbolic characters have
remarkable, im posing persona lities, while realistic characte rs develop
complex psychologies. Compare Batman (aka Bruce W ayne) to Saul
Goodman (aka Jimmy McGill).
3. Level of Conflict: Outer · Inner
Symbolic characters take action against external antagonistic social and
physical forces, while realistic characters often battle self-doubt, self-
deception, self-criticism, and other such inner confusions and invisible
demons.
4. Complexity: Solid · Dimensional
Like the archetypes that inspir e them, modern mythical characters have
traits bu t no dimensions, ó  but no contradictions, a text but no subtext.
They are one-piece symbols, inside and out.
Multidimensional realistic characters, on č hand, string dynamic
contradictions between their public, personal, private, and hidden á.
5. Detail: Scar city · Density
The Sym bolist w riter of fables, legends, and myths focuses ¯ essential.
He simplifies the density of Â by avoiding meticulou s traits of
í.
The Realist author , on č  hand, gathers å details th at enrich
and specify his characters. Com pare, ® the smooth outlines of
the animated family in The Incr edibles  Ñ painstakingly particularized
family in Six Feet Under .
6. Compr ehension: Hard · Easy
Realism calls for concentration and perception. ỉ ü real in
proportion to how much we consider his total self. The more c ontradictory
yet consistent, ě changeable yet unified, ě unpredictable yet
credible, ě specific yet mysterious the role, ě real,
fascinating, and  intriguing he À. He makes us work to  understand
him but rewards  us with insight  once we do. Consider V icomte  de V almont
( Danger ous Liaisons ), Thomas Sutpen ( Absalom, Absalom! ), and Jimmy
McGill ( Better Call Saul ).
The symbolic characters of my th, legend, and fable are insta ntly and
easily understood. The more generalized, predictable, and text-bound the
role, the less surprising, less intriguing, and less real he À. Consider
Albus Dumbledore, Mad Max, and Superman.
7. W orldview: Skeptical · Sentimental
Realism encour ages character s treated with tough-minded  sentiment;
Symbolism often sweetens its roles with sentimentality .
Sentiment is a fe eling that flows when a creditable motivation co mpels a
powerful action: a parent risking everything for a child, as in playwright
Henrik Ibsen’ s Ghosts  and Alvin Sar gent’ s screenplay for Or dinary People .
Sentimentality , è manipulates emotion by using phony causes to
trigger false ef fects. The forced happy ending, ® is a device as
antique as Hans Christian A ndersen’ s “The Little Mermaid” and as
yesterday as Steven Spielber g’ s W ar ¤ W orlds .
8. Endings: Ir ony · Simplicity
The writers of Realism grapple with life’ s constant duality: The steps you
take to achieve something are e xactly ¾ necessary to make sure you
never get it, while the steps you take to avoid something take you directly to
it. W hether climaxing in grief or joy , Realism turns a double ef fe ct: Positive
endings demand  great sacrifice, while tragedies bring insight and wisdom.
Reality is relentlessly ironic and characters ê fer accordingly .
W ith th e exception of authors like Philip K. Dick, myth, fable, and
legend r esist irony; they cast c haracters who act out straight, pure, and
positive stories.
9. Character Dynamics: Flexible · Rigid
Realism reveals  ảs hidd en in its characters Đ often flexes
their inner natures with change. Symbolic roles possess one rigid quality
through and through, ªrefore have nothing to reveal or change.
Realism is skeptical; myths wishful. T est myths against Â , ªir
archetypes are exposed Ú wish fulfillments they always were.
ë early twentieth century , psychologist Carl Jung based theories
about the collective unconscious on archetypes drawn from myths.
Expanding on Jung, Joseph Campbell concocted a monomyth popularly
known as “the hero’ s journey , ” hoping to replace Christianity with his
personal brand of spiritualism. Hollywood actionsmiths hammered
Campbell’ s pseudomyth about the hero’ s quest Í template to mass-
produce summer hits.5
10. Social Dynamics: Flexible · Rigid
The clas s structure in Symbolist genres tends to cast a ruler ² top,
peasants ² bottom, the res t somewhere in between. Realism moves
power through its cast with fluidity , even if ề is set in a monarchy
or dictatorship.
C A S E  S T U D Y :  G A M E  O F  T H R O N E S
David Beniof f and D. B. W eiss, adapting Geor ge R. R. Martin’ s A Song of
Ice and Fir e  to  the l ongform screen, brought harsh realism to high fantasy .
Between the black-and-white outlines of Symbolism, they drew characters
